    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "1. Stress and written accents"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        4.0,
        6.0,
        7.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Type",
        "Rule",
        "Examples"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "aguda (last syllable stressed)",
          "accent if it ends in vowel, -n or -s",
          "café, canción, estás; but: hotel, ciudad"
        ],
        [
          "llana (second-to-last stressed)",
          "accent if it does NOT end in vowel, -n, -s",
          "árbol, fácil, lápiz; but: casa, joven, crisis"
        ],
        [
          "esdrújula (third-to-last)",
          "always an accent",
          "teléfono, médico, rápido, música"
        ],
        [
          "sobresdrújula (fourth-to-last)",
          "always an accent",
          "cuéntaselo, dígamelo, llévatelo"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "Quick check",
      "text": "Say the word, find the stressed syllable, count from the end. <b>Agudas</b> and <b>llanas</b> get an accent only against the normal rule; <b>esdrújulas</b> always do."
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "A word <b>loses</b> or <b>gains</b> an accent when it changes: <i>joven / jóvenes, examen / exámenes, canción / canciones, régimen / regímenes</i>.",
        "<b>Compound adverbs in -mente</b> keep the accent of the adjective: <i>rápido: rápidamente, fácil: fácilmente</i>."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
